diff --git a/docs/internationalization.md b/docs/internationalization.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..eefac5b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/internationalization.md
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+# Languages
+
+Fizzy supports English (`en`, the default) and Japanese (`ja`). Visit any application
+page with `?locale=ja` or `?locale=en` to select a language. Unsupported values are
+ignored. Anonymous choices are kept in the Rails session; after authentication,
+the preference is stored on the global Identity and follows the person across
+accounts and browsers. No browser-language detection changes the English default.
+
+Application text lives in `config/locales/fizzy.en.yml` and `fizzy.ja.yml`; Rails
+Japanese date, duration, number, and validation strings live in `ja.yml`. Keep the
+English wording and formats unchanged when introducing translations. Use `t` for
+labels, placeholders, accessibility attributes, flash messages, and email subjects,
+and `l` for readable dates. Use interpolation rather than concatenating user data
+into translation keys. Only trusted HTML belongs in `_html` keys; escape dynamic
+values when constructing model-generated HTML.
+
+Browser controllers receive the `app.browser` catalog through page metadata.
+Do not translate the rich-text editor's packaged JavaScript strings. Brands,
+user-written content, protocol names, and internal identifiers are not translated.
+
+Requests and emails use scoped locales. Mailers resolve the recipient at rendering
+time, including queued delivery. Fragment caches and rendered Turbo streams are
+language-specific; shared refresh streams remain language-neutral. Browser page\nand offline caches are cleared when the language changes. Static errors
+include both languages because they cannot identify the viewer.
+
+Regression checks:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/current_locale_test.rb \
+  test/helpers/localization_test.rb test/mailers/localization_test.rb
+bin/rails test
+```
